===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|Britain}} A [[building]] or [[place]] [[house#Verb|housing]] [[shop]]s or [[stall]]s where all sorts of (especially [[exotic]]) [[manufactured]] [[article]]s are collected for sale. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=John Henry Newman|authorlink=John Henry Newman|chapter=Discourse V. General Knowledge Viewed as One Philosophy.|title=Discourses on the Scope and Nature of University Education. Addressed to the Catholics of Dublin|location=Dublin|publisher=[[w:James Duffy (Irish publisher)|James Duffy]], 7 Wellington Quay, publisher to His Grace the Catholic Archbishop of Dublin|year=1852|page=139|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=WL5bAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA139|oclc=2381364|passage=It is plain, that such writers do not rise to the very idea of a University. They consider it a sort of bazaar, or '''pantechnicon''', in which wares of all kinds are heaped together for sale in stalls independent of each other; and that, to save the purchasers the trouble of running about from shop to shop; or an hotel or lodging house, where all professions and classes are at liberty to congregate, varying, however, according to the season, each of them strange to each, and about its own work or pleasure; {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|Britain}} ''Originally'' '''pantechnicon van''': a [[van]], especially a large [[moving]] or [[removal van]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* '''1911''', {{w|Arnold Bennett}}, ''[[w:The Card|The Card: A Story of Adventure in the Five Towns]]'', London: {{w|Methuen Publishing}}, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/492063506 492063506]</small>; republished Toronto, Ont.: [[w:William Briggs (publisher)|William Briggs]], 1910s, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/225424669 225424669]</small>, [https://archive.org/stream/cardstoryofadven00bennuoft#page/69/mode/1up/ page 69]:
#*: The '''pantechnicon''' was running away. It had perceived the wrath to come and was fleeing. Its guardians had evidently left it imperfectly scotched or braked, and it had got loose. {{...}} [T]he onrush of the '''pantechnicon''' constituted a clear crisis. Lower down the gradient of Brougham Street was more dangerous, and it was within the possibilities that people inhabiting the depths of the street might find themselves pitched out of bed by the sharp corner of a '''pantechnicon''' that was determined to be a '''pantechnicon'''.
#* {{quote-book|author=William Barclay|authorlink=William Barclay (theologian)|chapter=The Race and the Goal|title=The Letter to the Hebrews|series=The Daily Study Bible Series|edition=rev.|location=Louisville, Ky.|publisher=[[w:Westminster John Knox|Westminster John Knox Press]]|year=1972|page=172|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=LcXwedfaPhUC&pg=PA172|isbn=978-0-664-21312-1|passage=In the Christian life we have ''a handicap''. If we are encircled by the greatness of the past, we are also encircled by the handicap of our own sin. No man would seek to climb Mount Everest with a '''pantechnicon''' of lumber weighing him down. If we would travel far, we must travel light.}}
#* '''1978''', {{w|Lawrence Durrell}}, ''[[w:Livia (novel)|Livia: Or Buried Alive: A Novel]]'', London: {{w|Faber and Faber}}, <small>{{ISBN|978-0-571-11297-5}}</small>; republished in ''[[w:The Avignon Quintet|The Avignon Quintet: Monsieur, Livia, Constance, Sebastian, Quinx]]'', London: Faber and Faber, 1992, <small>{{ISBN|978-0-571-16328-1}}</small>, page 426:
#*: In fact, as they later found, the auxiliary vehicle was a very large removers' van&nbsp;– the kind known as a '''pantechnicon'''.
#* {{quote-book|author=Anthony Trethowan|chapter=Wendy Atkinson|title=Delta Scout: Ground Coverage Operator|location=Johannesburg|publisher=30° South Publishers|year=2008|page=30|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=cCCiE0_RDSMC&pg=PA30|isbn=978-1-920143-21-3|passage=I later established that the R12 &#91;{{w|Renault 12}}&#93; and '''pantechnicon''' had both been driving in the direction of the roadblock, the R12 behind the '''pantechnicon'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=E[dward] H[ector] Burn|author2=J[ohn] Cartwright|authorlink2=John Cartwright (lawyer)|chapter=Adverse Possession and Limitation of Actions|title=Maudsley and Burn's Land Law: Cases & Materials|edition=9th|location=Oxford; New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Oxford University Press}}|year=2009|pages=291–292|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=3YWcAQAAQBAJ&pg=PA292|isbn=978-0-19-922617-7|passage={{...}} ''Bulstrode v Lambert'' [1953] 1 WLR 1064 {{...}} [T]he plaintiff made use of the route with furniture vans and '''pantechnicons''' for bringing goods to the mart. The defendant objected on the ground that the parked '''pantechnicons''' interfered with his business as a cafe and car hire proprietor, but [[w:Gerald Upjohn, Baron Upjohn|Upjohn]] J held that the plaintiff could use the way with '''pantechnicons''' which could park as long as was necessary for loading and unloading, this being an incident of a right of way.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=John D. Beasley|chapter=What a Lot of Shops and Stalls!|title=East Dulwich Remembered|location=Stroud, Gloucestershire|publisher=Amberley Publishing|year=2010|isbn=978-1-4456-0138-0|passage=Shinkfield's had at one time belonged to a firm called Hutchison's which did furniture removals. They stabled their horses and '''pantechnicon''' in the yard which runs behind the Lord Palmerston and the adjoining shops, with access from North Cross Road.}}

